, \\mathrm{x}=2$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213b4d91ecd39cf9955"},{"id":51278,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"$\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ reacts with","options":["only water","only acids","only alkalis","both acids and alkalis"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is amphoteric so it dissolve in acid as well in alkalis","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213c43257f7093a7b8c"},{"id":51279,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Identify the cyclic silicate ion given in the figure below\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FYlMOslBG9oJ90rnao4xRRVIu1tf6Q79xDMdCdd4BPyE.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FYlMOslBG9oJ90rnao4xRRVIu1tf6Q79xDMdCdd4BPyE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FYlMOslBG9oJ90rnao4xRRVIu1tf6Q79xDMdCdd4BPyE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$\\left[\\mathrm{Si}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{25}\\right]^{24-}$","$\\left[\\mathrm{Si}_{6} \\mathrm{O}_{18}\\right]^{18-}$","$\\left[\\mathrm{Si}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{12}\\right]^{12}$","$\\left[\\mathrm{S}_{6} \\mathrm{O}_{24}\\right]^{12-}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"Cyclic or ring silicates have general formula\n\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\n\\left(\\mathrm{Si} \\cdot \\mathrm{O}_{3}{ }^{2-}\\right)_{\\mathrm{n}} & \\text { or }\\left(\\mathrm{Si} \\cdot \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right) \\mathrm{n}^{2 \\mathrm{n}-} \\\\\n\\downarrow & \\downarrow \\\\\n\\mathrm{S}_{3} \\mathrm{O}_{9}{ }^{6-} & \\mathrm{S}_{6} \\mathrm{O}_{18}^{12-}\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2130e176a9f161743a8"},{"id":51280,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The tendency of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BX</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>Cl</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>OMe</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>NMe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> to form a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>π</mi></math>-bond with boron follows the order","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OMe</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NMe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OMe</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NMe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NMe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OMe</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NMe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OMe</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>Boron atom has an incomplete octet. It can accept a lone pair from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> given in the question and can also donate electron to it, thus it can form a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>π</mi></math>-bond also known as back bonding.</p>\n\n<p>The extent of back bonding depends upon the electronegativity of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> and on the size of the valence shell. Lesser is the electronegativity of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> and similar is the size of valence shell of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>, more will be the extend of back bonding.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cl</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math> are more electronegative than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>OMe</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NMe</mi></math> group, so there extent of back bonding will be least. Among <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>Cl</mi></math> will have least tendency to form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>π</mi></math>-bond with boron, because of large size of its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math>-orbital.</p>\n\n<p>Among <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OMe</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NMe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>NMe</mi></math><br />\nwill have the maximum tendency to form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>π</mi></math>-bond because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NMe</mi></math> is less electronegative than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>OMe</mi></math> group. Thus, the tendency to form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>π</mi></math>-bond with boron follows the order.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OMe</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NMe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da68bf2651ba936020a51"}]}